  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking",
  "text": "Here's a comprehensive overview.\n\n---\n\n## Grafana Codebase Overview\n\nGrafana is a large monorepo (~2M+ lines) with a **Go backend** and a **React/TypeScript frontend**, plus a set of shared npm packages published under `@grafana/*`.\n\n### High-Level Structure\n\n| Directory | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `pkg/` | Go backend — API handlers, services, data models, infrastructure, plugin system |\n| `public/app/` | React frontend — the single-page application |\n| `packages/` | Shared npm packages (`@grafana/ui`, `@grafana/data`, `@grafana/runtime`, etc.) |\n| `apps/` | Kubernetes API server applications (newer resource-style APIs) |\n| `e2e/`, `e2e-playwright/` | End-to-end tests |\n| `conf/`, `devenv/` | Config and dev environment |\n\n### Backend (`pkg/`)\n\n- **`pkg/api/`** — HTTP API handlers and route registration\n- **`pkg/services/`** — 70+ business-logic services (dashboards, alerting, users, auth, datasources, etc.)\n- **`pkg/infra/`** — Infrastructure (DB, logging, tracing, caching, HTTP clients)\n- **`pkg/apis/`** — Kubernetes-style API definitions (the \"resource API\" migration)\n- **`pkg/registry/`** — Wire dependency injection registration\n- **`pkg/plugins/`** — Plugin lifecycle management\n\n### Frontend (`public/app/`)\n\n- **`core/`** — Shared components, services, hooks, navigation, Redux reducers\n- **`features/`** — 50+ feature modules (dashboards, alerting, explore, plugins, etc.)\n- **`routes/`** — Route definitions and the router wrapper\n- **`plugins/`** — Built-in panel and datasource plugins\n- **`store/`** — Redux store setup\n\n### Shared Packages (`packages/`)\n\nKey ones: `grafana-ui` (component library), `grafana-data` (data types/DataFrames), `grafana-runtime` (runtime services including `locationService`), `grafana-schema` (JSON schemas), `grafana-api-clients`.\n\n---\n\n## React Router Architecture (in detail)\n\nThis is the most nuanced part of the codebase. Grafana is **mid-migration** from React Router v5 to v6. Here's the full picture:\n\n### Dependencies\n\n```\nreact-router: 5.3.4          (v5 — still the primary router)\nreact-router-dom: 5.3.4      (v5 DOM bindings)\nreact-router-dom-v5-compat: ^6.26.1  (v6 compat shim)\nhistory: 4.10.1               (v4 — the history library used by RR v5)\n```\n\n### The Router Tree\n\nThe component hierarchy looks like this:\n\n```\nAppWrapper (class component)\n  └─ RouterWrapper (in RoutesWrapper.tsx)\n       └─ <Router history={locationService.getHistory()}>    ← RR v5 Router\n            └─ <LocationServiceProvider>\n                 └─ <CompatRouter>                           ← v5-compat bridge\n                      └─ <AppChrome>\n                           └─ <Routes>                       ← RR v6 <Routes>\n                                └─ <Route element={<GrafanaRouteWrapper>} />  (per route)\n```\n\nKey file: `public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx`\n\n```1:55:public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx\nimport { ComponentType, ReactNode, type JSX } from 'react';\nimport { Router } from 'react-router-dom';\nimport { CompatRouter } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\n// ... providers and chrome wrapping\n```\n\nThe **v5 `<Router>`** is the root, receiving a `history` instance from `locationService`. Then `<CompatRouter>` from `react-router-dom-v5-compat` bridges the gap so that v6-style `<Routes>` and `<Route>` components work inside it. `AppWrapper` renders routes using the v6 API:\n\n```80:93:public/app/AppWrapper.tsx\n  renderRoute = (route: RouteDescriptor) => {\n    return (\n      <Route\n        caseSensitive={route.sensitive === undefined ? false : route.sensitive}\n        path={route.path}\n        key={route.path}\n        element={<GrafanaRouteWrapper route={route} />}\n      />\n    );\n  };\n\n  renderRoutes() {\n    return <Routes>{getAppRoutes().map((r) => this.renderRoute(r))}</Routes>;\n  }\n```\n\n### Route Definitions\n\nRoutes are defined as an array of `RouteDescriptor` objects in `public/app/routes/routes.tsx`. The type is:\n\n```15:30:public/app/core/navigation/types.ts\nexport interface RouteDescriptor {\n  path: string;\n  component: GrafanaRouteComponent;\n  roles?: () => string[];\n  pageClass?: string;\n  routeName?: string;\n  chromeless?: boolean;\n  sensitive?: boolean;\n  allowAnonymous?: boolean | ((params: Readonly<Params<string>>) => boolean);\n}\n```\n\nThe `getAppRoutes()` function assembles all routes — including sub-routes from features like alerting, plugins, connections, provisioning, and profiles — into a flat array. Most components are loaded lazily via `SafeDynamicImport` (webpack dynamic imports with code splitting).\n\n### GrafanaRoute / GrafanaRouteWrapper\n\nEvery route renders through `GrafanaRouteWrapper` → `GrafanaRoute` (in `public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx`). This wrapper:\n\n1. **Auth checks** — Redirects to `/login` if not signed in, checks role-based access\n2. **Chrome setup** — Calls `chrome.setMatchedRoute()` to configure the app shell\n3. **Keybindings** — Initializes global keyboard shortcuts per route\n4. **DOM cleanup** — Manages body class names, removes stale tooltips\n5. **Pageview tracking** — Reports analytics on navigation\n6. **Error boundaries** — Wraps each route in `<ErrorBoundary>` with `<Suspense>` for lazy loading\n\n### The Two Navigation Paradigms (Migration State)\n\nThis is where the active migration lives. There are **two ways** navigation happens:\n\n#### 1. `locationService` (Legacy — still dominant)\n\nDefined in `packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx`, this is a singleton service wrapping the `history` v4 library:\n\n```16:31:packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx\nexport interface LocationService {\n  partial: (query: Record<string, any>, replace?: boolean) => void;\n  push: (location: H.Path | H.LocationDescriptor<any>) => void;\n  replace: (location: H.Path | H.LocationDescriptor<any>) => void;\n  reload: () => void;\n  getLocation: () => H.Location;\n  getHistory: () => H.History;\n  getSearch: () => URLSearchParams;\n  getSearchObject: () => UrlQueryMap;\n  getLocationObservable: () => Observable<H.Location>;\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nUsed extensively throughout the codebase — `locationService.push(...)`, `locationService.partial(...)`, `locationService.replace(...)`. There are **hundreds** of call sites across the frontend. This works because the v5 `<Router>` at the root receives `locationService.getHistory()`, so any imperative pushes through this service are picked up by the router.\n\n#### 2. `useNavigate` (Modern — the migration target)\n\nThe React Router v6 `useNavigate` hook, imported from `react-router-dom-v5-compat`. Currently used in ~25 files, primarily in newer features like **provisioning**, **alerting**, and **admin**. This is the idiomatic v6 approach.\n\n#### Migration Status\n\nThe migration from `locationService` to `useNavigate` is **in early stages**:\n- **`locationService.push/replace/partial`** — still used in ~200+ files across the app\n- **`useNavigate`** — adopted in ~25 files, mostly in newer/recently-refactored code (provisioning, some alerting, admin)\n- **`react-router-dom` (v5 direct)** — only 3 files still import from the v5 package directly (the router itself, notifications, and explore test setup)\n- **`react-router-dom-v5-compat`** — ~100+ files use v6-style hooks (`useLocation`, `useParams`, `Navigate`, `useNavigate`) through the compat layer\n\nThe compat layer (`CompatRouter`) is the bridge that makes this work — it allows v6 hooks inside a v5 router tree. The eventual goal is to remove the v5 `<Router>` and `locationService` singleton entirely, replacing them with a proper v6 `createBrowserRouter` or similar, but that hasn't happened yet.\n\n### How Route Props Flow\n\nUnlike standard React Router where components get route props directly, Grafana wraps everything through `GrafanaRouteWrapper`:\n\n```64:89:public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx\nexport function GrafanaRouteWrapper({ route }: Pick<Props, 'route'>) {\n  const location = useLocation();\n  const params = useParams();\n  // ... auth checks ...\n  return <GrafanaRoute route={route} location={location} />;\n}\n```\n\nRoute components receive a `GrafanaRouteComponentProps` object with `route`, `queryParams`, and `location` — not the standard React Router props. This abstraction layer is what makes the migration possible without touching every page component.\n\n### Key Architectural Takeaways\n\n1. **Hybrid v5/v6 setup** — v5 `<Router>` at the root, `<CompatRouter>` bridge, v6 `<Routes>/<Route>` for rendering\n2. **`locationService` is the dominant navigation mechanism** — a singleton wrapping `history` v4, used imperatively throughout the app (including in non-React code like services and Redux actions)\n3. **`useNavigate` is the migration target** — gradually being adopted in newer code\n4. **Flat route array** — all routes are defined declaratively in `getAppRoutes()`, composed from feature-specific route functions\n5. **`GrafanaRouteWrapper`** abstracts route rendering, handling auth, chrome config, and analytics uniformly\n6. **Lazy loading everywhere** — `SafeDynamicImport` wraps all page-level imports for code splitting",
